<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1199–<a href="/years/1349">1349</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Principality of Galicia–Volhynia, Kingdom of Ruthenia, Kingdom of Rus'</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, also known as the Kingdom of Ruthenia, was a medieval state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, mostly in modern-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and one of the three most important powers to emerge from the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman the Great</a> united the principalities of Galicia and Volhynia at the turn of the 13th century. After the Mongol invasion of 1239–1241, Prince Daniel of Galicia pledged allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1246, and in 1253 he was crowned King of Ruthenia by the Pope in an attempt to create an anti-Mongol alliance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> annexed Galicia in 1349, and Volhynia came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> may have emerged in the late 10th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a>, a great-great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir II Monomakh</a>, inherited its throne in 1170. The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was formed between 1124 and 1144 when Vladimirko Volodarovich united Zvenyhorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Peremyshl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terebovlia">Terebovlia</a>. The two were joined after the last Prince of Galicia, Vladimir II Yaroslavich, died without a recognised heir in the paternal line. Roman acquired Galicia at the invitation of the boyars, who expected an absentee prince, but he curbed their power and expelled those who opposed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Roman was killed by Polish forces at Zawichost in 1205, and nearly 40 years of rebellion followed. Daniel travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and in 1246 acknowledged, at least nominally, the supremacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Vernadsky saw a tactical alliance within a long-term strategy of resistance, while Magocsi argued that Daniel was a Mongol vassal who collected Mongol tribute. Plans for an anti-Mongol coalition led to Daniel's coronation by a papal legate in 1253. His son Leo moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> in 1272. Decline followed Leo's death in 1301, and the brothers Andrew and Leo II died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1323 without heirs, ending the Rurikid line there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After wars among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, Casimir III captured and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in 1349. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> came under Lithuanian control and formally became a Lithuanian province in 1452. Casimir took the title King of Poland and Ruthenia, and in 1434 the territory became the Ruthenian Voivodeship, whose last legal autonomy was abolished in 1506. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a> had styled himself king of Galicia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, a title the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> later adopted. The economy rested on agriculture, crafts and salt, and its towns profited from trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
